The decision rule is clear: volatile works when exactly **one thread writes** and the write is an independent assignment (not dependent on the current value).

**Safe with volatile**: a shutdown flag. One thread sets `volatile boolean running = false;` and worker threads check it. No read-modify-write, no compound operation.

```java
volatile boolean running = true;

// Worker thread
while (running) { doWork(); }

// Shutdown thread
running = false; // single write, no dependency on current value
```

**Not safe with volatile**: a counter. Multiple threads increment `count++`. Even though each read sees the latest value (volatile visibility), two threads can read the same value and write the same incremented value — one increment is lost.

**Not safe with volatile**: check-then-act. `if (instance == null) { instance = new Singleton(); }` — two threads can both see null, both create instances. This is why **double-checked locking** requires volatile for the instance field plus synchronized for the check-then-act block.

The double-checked locking pattern relies on volatile for a subtle reason: without it, the JIT can reorder the Singleton constructor so that the instance reference is assigned before the constructor finishes — another thread sees a non-null but partially constructed object.
